{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Knot.KashaevEndpointSplit","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Knot.KashaevEndpointSplit","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Knot.KashaevReflectionTrapezoid"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.FigureEightVolume.sum_log_two_sin_split","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.FigureEightVolume.sum_log_two_sin_split","kind":"theorem","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/KashaevEndpointSplit.lean#L87-L141","doc":"**The endpoint split.**\n\n    ∑_{j=1}^{k} log(2 sin(jπ/N)) = k·log(2π/N) + log(k!) + ∑_{j=1}^{k} logSinc(jπ/N)\n\nexact, for every `N ≠ 0` and every `k < N`.  Each term is\n`log(2 sin x) = log(2π/N) + log j + logSinc x` at `x = jπ/N`; summing the\n`log j` gives `log(k!)`.\n\nThis is the step that removes the `j = 0` singularity from (A): the left side is\nsingular termwise as `j/N → 0`, the right side is a closed form plus a factorial\nplus a sum of a function continuous at `0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.FigureEightVolume.logSinc_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.FigureEightVolume.logSinc_neg","kind":"theorem","line":81,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/KashaevEndpointSplit.lean#L81-L85","doc":"**`logSinc` is even.**  Hence every odd derivative vanishes at `0`, which is\nwhy the Euler–Maclaurin expansion of `∑ logSinc(jh)` has no `h`-order endpoint\ncontribution at the left end — the reason the split works. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.FigureEightVolume.logSinc_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.FigureEightVolume.logSinc_zero","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/KashaevEndpointSplit.lean#L74-L79","doc":"`logSinc 0 = 0` — the singularity really is gone (`Real.log 0 = 0` in\nMathlib's convention, and `sin 0 = 0`, so both terms vanish).  The analytic\ncontent is that `log(sin x / x) → 0` as `x → 0`; this is the value the\ncontinuous extension takes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.FigureEightVolume.logSinc","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.FigureEightVolume.logSinc","kind":"def","line":69,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/KashaevEndpointSplit.lean#L69-L72","doc":"`logSinc x = log(sin x) − log x`, i.e. `log(sin x / x)` where that is\ndefined.  The regular part of `log(2 sin)` after the `log(2x)` singularity is\nsubtracted. ","signatureChunk":0}]}